  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA strong PV anomaly dropping southeast through Missouri will merge \nwith a Gulf Coast states upper-level wave over the TN Valley on \nThursday. The resulting large low pressure center then takes a \nnortheast pivot up the western spine of the Appalachians. This \nevolution will eventually bring a soaking rain north into the local \narea late Thursday morning into Thursday afternoon, with periods of \nlight-moderate rain persisting within the system deformation zone \ninto Thursday night and Friday morning. A solid 0.50\" to 1.00\" of \nrain appears likely, heaviest along/south of US 24 where heavier 1\" \nplus totals remain possible. Higher theta-e air riding up the \nisentropic surfaces should support this blossoming rain shield on \nthe northern fringe of this system, with locally heavy rainfall \nrates Thursday PM maximizes in more robust frontogenetic banding. \nThermals could cool just enough in spots late Thursday night into \nFriday morning for wet snow to mix in. Not expecting accums/impacts \nat this time. \n\nThe upper level pattern will remain active this weekend \nas several faster moving shortwaves track east within aggregate \ntroughing over the lower 48. A quick moving, moist-starved shortwave \ntakes aim on the lower Great Lakes Saturday morning. The lacking \nmoisture and weak forcing with the wave precludes a mentionable PoP \nat this time. A larger scale upper trough and developing sfc \nreflection brings the next good chance for rain Sunday afternoon \ninto Sunday evening. There could be enough cold air on the backside \nof the sfc low for precip to end as wet snow, though confidence in \nreceiving snow is very low. \n\nA flatter, more zonal flow regime looks to settle in for the first \nhalf of next week with dry conditions and gradual moderation.",
